      "lines": [
        {
          "x": 126,
          "y": 58,
          "width": 144,
          "height": 28,
          "text": "PART B2",
          "bold": true
        },
        {
          "x": 125,
          "y": 130,
          "width": 961,
          "height": 29,
          "text": "Read Text 5 and answer questions 43-64 in the Question-Answer Book for Part B2.",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 124,
          "y": 199,
          "width": 118,
          "height": 28,
          "text": "Text 5",
          "bold": true
        },
        {
          "x": 137,
          "y": 278,
          "width": 271,
          "height": 41,
          "text": "Quiet Quitting",
          "bold": true
        },
        {
          "x": 125,
          "y": 340,
          "width": 1327,
          "height": 38,
          "text": "[1] Quiet quitting is a buzzy term doing the rounds today. In fact, a Google search produces hits from The",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 126,
          "y": 379,
          "width": 1325,
          "height": 30,
          "text": "Washington Post, The New York Times, The Wall Street Journal, and countless academic publications. At this",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 124,
          "y": 413,
          "width": 1333,
          "height": 29,
          "text": "moment, at least 50% of the North American workforce is engaged in a wave of disengagement dubbed quiet quitting.",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 125,
          "y": 444,
          "width": 1325,
          "height": 38,
          "text": "But what does it mean and how will it impact university graduates as they prepare to embark on 21st-century careers?",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 124,
          "y": 498,
          "width": 308,
          "height": 30,
          "text": "What Is quiet quitting?",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 126,
          "y": 545,
          "width": 1326,
          "height": 37,
          "text": "[2] As many have noted the term itself is misleading. No, it does not refer to wordlessly slipping out the door or",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 125,
          "y": 584,
          "width": 1327,
          "height": 29,
          "text": "logging out of the group chat without so much as a peace sign emoji. Nor does it mean an employee permanently",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 124,
          "y": 614,
          "width": 1328,
          "height": 38,
          "text": "abandoning their post. Quiet quitters believe a more accurate way to describe this is working your contract or acting",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 124,
          "y": 653,
          "width": 1328,
          "height": 30,
          "text": "your wage. Carrie Bulger, PhD, a professor of organizational psychology, defines quiet quitting as “a renewed effort",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 45,
          "y": 687,
          "width": 28,
          "height": 23,
          "text": "10",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 124,
          "y": 687,
          "width": 481,
          "height": 29,
          "text": "to really focus on what a job is and isn’t.”",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 125,
          "y": 738,
          "width": 1327,
          "height": 30,
          "text": "[3] No matter what terminology we use, quiet quitting is less a harbinger of widespread revolt and more of a [ong",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 124,
          "y": 772,
          "width": 1330,
          "height": 30,
          "text": "overdue reckoning with outdated practices and stale office culture. “For some people, it’s probably a sign of",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 124,
          "y": 807,
          "width": 1277,
          "height": 29,
          "text": "dissatisfaction and a sign that they’re going to leave,” Bulger said. “But I think for others, it’s something else.”",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 125,
          "y": 858,
          "width": 1325,
          "height": 30,
          "text": "[4] Being forced to work at home amid the tumult of 2020 was an eye-opening experience, one that emboldened",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 45,
          "y": 892,
          "width": 27,
          "height": 24,
          "text": "15",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 124,
          "y": 893,
          "width": 1327,
          "height": 29,
          "text": "even the most dedicated employees to set healthy boundaries. And enforcing those boundaries can appear, through",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 124,
          "y": 927,
          "width": 1327,
          "height": 30,
          "text": "the lens of leadership, like slacking. “But it is quite possible to be invested in your work and redirect that passion",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 124,
          "y": 961,
          "width": 1327,
          "height": 30,
          "text": "once you clock out for the day. Pursuing work-life enrichment prioritizes self-care in one area and tends to breed",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 124,
          "y": 996,
          "width": 390,
          "height": 28,
          "text": "success in the other,” Bulger said.",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 125,
          "y": 1047,
          "width": 319,
          "height": 28,
          "text": "Going against the grind",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 43,
          "y": 1097,
          "width": 31,
          "height": 23,
          "text": "20",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 126,
          "y": 1097,
          "width": 1325,
          "height": 30,
          "text": "[5] The signs are all around us — is no longer seen as the badge of honor it once was, and Generation",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 125,
          "y": 1132,
          "width": 1327,
          "height": 30,
          "text": "Z are starting to question how relevant it is while seeking better frameworks that support the kind of life they want",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 125,
          "y": 1167,
          "width": 1327,
          "height": 29,
          "text": "to live. Launched in 2013, one online community that exists for people who ‘want help with their own work-life",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 125,
          "y": 1201,
          "width": 1327,
          "height": 30,
          "text": "related struggles’ saw an upsurge in followers in late 2021. The Instagram account The Nap Ministry has a similar",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 124,
          "y": 1236,
          "width": 1331,
          "height": 29,
          "text": "popularity arc and saw a huge explosion of followers since the start of the pandemic, numbering in the tens of",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 124,
          "y": 1270,
          "width": 1326,
          "height": 29,
          "text": "thousands per day. Tricia Hersey, its creator, believes Gen Z work hard; they just don’t want to live to work. To her,",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 125,
          "y": 1305,
          "width": 819,
          "height": 28,
          "text": "and to many others, it is about “not running ourselves into the ground.”",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 126,
          "y": 1352,
          "width": 1326,
          "height": 38,
          "text": "[6] Quiet quitting is only one note in this growing chorus, and we should be careful not to dismiss it as just another",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 120,
          "y": 1387,
          "width": 1337,
          "height": 38,
          "text": "trend or a singular occurrence. Instead, we should step back and look at the whole picture and what that means for",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 124,
          "y": 1422,
          "width": 739,
          "height": 37,
          "text": "the way we've been working and how we should move forward.",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 126,
          "y": 1477,
          "width": 1325,
          "height": 30,
          "text": "[7] There are, of course, divergent perspectives on quiet quitting. There’s what it means for workers and what it",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 125,
          "y": 1512,
          "width": 1327,
          "height": 30,
          "text": "means for panicked employers in a precarious economy, and the collision of those viewpoints can have drastic",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 124,
          "y": 1546,
          "width": 1331,
          "height": 30,
          "text": "consequences for the former. Writing for The Wall Street Journal, columnist Callum Borchers outlined the perils of",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 125,
          "y": 1581,
          "width": 1327,
          "height": 30,
          "text": "quiet quitting. Borchers has encountered a new term quict firing in which reputations suffer as a result of perceived",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 125,
          "y": 1615,
          "width": 1326,
          "height": 31,
          "text": "apathy; responsibilities are gradually diminished and a path toward actual termination materializes, “Companies",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 43,
          "y": 1650,
          "width": 31,
          "height": 23,
          "text": "35",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 124,
          "y": 1650,
          "width": 1328,
          "height": 30,
          "text": "have always had subtle ways to nudge people out the door,” Borchers said. “Tactics include sidelining them by",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 125,
          "y": 1684,
          "width": 1189,
          "height": 30,
          "text": "cutting responsibilities or denying promotions and raises to make someone miserable enough to leave.”",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 126,
          "y": 1737,
          "width": 1326,
          "height": 30,
          "text": "[8] Gergo Vari, CEO of online job searching platform Lensa, also believes the decision won’t serve employees long-",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 125,
          "y": 1772,
          "width": 1327,
          "height": 30,
          "text": "term cither. “Anytime that you silence your own voice in an organization, you may be depriving yourself of the",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 125,
          "y": 1806,
          "width": 1327,
          "height": 30,
          "text": "opportunity to change that organization.” Stepping away from hustle culture can also cause tension between",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 125,
          "y": 1837,
          "width": 1327,
          "height": 38,
          "text": "employees and may result in a rift between fellow colleagues who may have to pick up the slack. “If people feel like",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 125,
          "y": 1875,
          "width": 1196,
          "height": 30,
          "text": "their co-workers aren’t committed to quality work there can be friction inside teams and organizations.”",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 124,
          "y": 1926,
          "width": 290,
          "height": 29,
          "text": "A generational divide",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 126,
          "y": 1978,
          "width": 1326,
          "height": 30,
          "text": "[9] Today's college students are rightly praised for their resiliency. But the same skills that made them so adaptable",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 125,
          "y": 2009,
          "width": 1327,
          "height": 38,
          "text": "during the pandemic — they are digital natives who prefer Zoom lectures to the real thing —- make them the target",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 125,
          "y": 2044,
          "width": 1327,
          "height": 37,
          "text": "of generational disdain once they enter the workforce, Can’t you just hear the grumbling: We wouldn’t have to worry",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 43,
          "y": 2048,
          "width": 30,
          "height": 23,
          "text": "45",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 126,
          "y": 2083,
          "width": 1326,
          "height": 28,
          "text": "about quiet quitting if these kids would get off TikTok and do their jobs. Fittingly, the movement’s origins can be",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 132,
          "y": 2178,
          "width": 729,
          "height": 21,
          "text": "2025-DSE-ENG LANG 1-B2-RP-2 2",
          "bold": true
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "width": 1500,
      "height": 1340,
      "label": "Reading passage page 2",
      "lines": [
        {
          "x": 113,
          "y": 63,
          "width": 1343,
          "height": 31,
          "text": "traced to TikTok, the massively popular video-sharing app, where the influencer Zaid Khan uttered a phrase that’s",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 113,
          "y": 97,
          "width": 1098,
          "height": 31,
          "text": "been quoted in countless news articles; “Your worth as a person is not defined by your labor.”",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 114,
          "y": 146,
          "width": 1342,
          "height": 39,
          "text": "[10] In an essay for The New Yorker, Cal Newport, an associate professor of computer science, posits that Gen Z is",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 113,
          "y": 185,
          "width": 1343,
          "height": 31,
          "text": "better equipped than its forebears to leverage technology to push back against workplace norms. Gen Z, enters the",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 113,
          "y": 220,
          "width": 1343,
          "height": 31,
          "text": "workforce with “a mindset that is notably distinct from the millennials who preceded them,” Newport writes. As the",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 113,
          "y": 255,
          "width": 1343,
          "height": 32,
          "text": "first group to fully come of age with smartphones and social media, Gen Z “formed an understanding of the world",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 113,
          "y": 290,
          "width": 1342,
          "height": 31,
          "text": "in which the boundaries between the digital and real are blurred.” They have so thoroughly mixed work and self, the",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 113,
          "y": 321,
          "width": 1345,
          "height": 39,
          "text": "suffocating grimness of modern office life hits a more personal level and it has become clear to many that they need",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 113,
          "y": 360,
          "width": 514,
          "height": 29,
          "text": "to separate their personhood from their jobs.",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 113,
          "y": 408,
          "width": 1343,
          "height": 38,
          "text": "{11] Work, of course, remains essential within our current economic and social environment and trends like quiet",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 113,
          "y": 443,
          "width": 1345,
          "height": 38,
          "text": "quitting are inevitable during times of great upheaval. What we need to do is take a step back and take a critical look",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 109,
          "y": 478,
          "width": 1345,
          "height": 38,
          "text": "at how we can adapt the way we work to suit our current context while promoting a more modern and balanced way",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 113,
          "y": 516,
          "width": 1341,
          "height": 31,
          "text": "of life, As Barnaby Lashbrooke wrote in Forbes: “Of course we have to work: to put a roof overhead and food on",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 111,
          "y": 551,
          "width": 811,
          "height": 27,
          "text": "the table, But life should come first, and work should be the enabler.”",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 127,
          "y": 638,
          "width": 140,
          "height": 23,
          "text": "Comments:",
          "bold": true
        },
        {
          "x": 128,
          "y": 690,
          "width": 353,
          "height": 28,
          "text": "Nikia203 | April 2025 10:45",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 127,
          "y": 718,
          "width": 1260,
          "height": 38,
          "text": "Figuring out how work fits into a life well lived is hard, but I think it’s an evolution that has to happen. The",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 127,
          "y": 752,
          "width": 336,
          "height": 37,
          "text": "question is what comes next.",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 128,
          "y": 806,
          "width": 352,
          "height": 30,
          "text": "Lewis43 | April 2025 12:23",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 127,
          "y": 840,
          "width": 1279,
          "height": 31,
          "text": "I'm not going to put in a sixty-hour workweek and pull myself up by my bootstraps for a job that doesn’t care",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 127,
          "y": 873,
          "width": 248,
          "height": 28,
          "text": "about me as a person.",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 126,
          "y": 925,
          "width": 353,
          "height": 28,
          "text": "Jessef04 1 April 2025 14:47",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 122,
          "y": 953,
          "width": 967,
          "height": 38,
          "text": "If you're doing this, you're a loser. This is like a virus. This is worse than COVID.",
          "bold": false
        },
        {
          "x": 285,
          "y": 1008,
